    dict(key='06-three-guards', kind='figure', title='One nightly backup, and the guards around it',
         body='three_guards',
         narration="Let's see the whole idea on one real example: the shop's nightly backup. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the backup job. [[slnc 300]] It has its own key. [[slnc 600]] Guard one, in blue: the key may only add. [[slnc 300]] Put object, and nothing else. [[slnc 300]] Reading and deleting are refused. [[slnc 600]] Guard two, in cyan: a checksum travels with the bytes. [[slnc 300]] A good upload is allowed. [[slnc 300]] A damaged one is refused. [[slnc 600]] Guard three, in amber: versions. [[slnc 300]] An overwrite adds a version, and the old ones stay. [[slnc 600]] Guard four, in green: the lock. [[slnc 300]] Until its date, even the root account cannot delete the backup. [[slnc 600]] The five demos take these guards one at a time."),
    dict(key='07-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title='The versions of orders.csv, step by step',
         body='versions',
         narration='First demo: versioning. [[slnc 400]] Each column is one step. [[slnc 300]] The boxes are the versions of the orders file, newest on top. [[slnc 600]] First, the shop writes its orders. [[slnc 300]] One version, forty bytes. [[slnc 600]] Then ransomware writes scrambled bytes. [[slnc 300]] On a plain disk, that would replace the file. [[slnc 300]] Here it becomes version two, and version one is still underneath. [[slnc 600]] The restore copies version one back, as version three. [[slnc 300]] A normal read sees the orders again. [[slnc 300]] The scrambled version stays, as evidence. [[slnc 600]] Last, a plain delete. [[slnc 300]] It only adds a delete marker on top. [[slnc 300]] A normal read now fails, with four oh four. [[slnc 300]] But nothing below is gone.'),
